            <ArticleTitle>Treatment of Kaposi Sarcoma Herpesvirus-Associated Multicentric Castleman Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>75-88</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8588(17)30147-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hoc.2017.09.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus (KSHV)-associated multicentric Castleman disease (MCD) is a rare, polyclonal lymphoproliferative disorder characterized by flares of inflammatory symptoms, edema, cytopenias, lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly. Diagnosis requires a lymph node biopsy. Pathogenesis is related to dysregulated inflammatory cytokines, including human and viral interleukin-6. Rituximab alone or in combination with chemotherapy, such as liposomal doxorubicin, has led to an overall survival of over 90% at 5 years. Experimental approaches to treatment include virus activated cytotoxic therapy with high-dose zidovudine and valganciclovir and targeting human interleukin-6 activity. Despite successful treatment of KSHV-MCD, patients remain at high risk for developing non-Hodgkin lymphomas.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
